        - VERIFIED, no change: the enum comment's "the struct is shared by four handlers" (kimi substitute
          asked for its warrant) -- `d->warned' is written or read in exactly four functions
          (DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_re), DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_de), draw_primitive, horrible_getputpixel), by awk over
          the file.  "the two demotions above it" refers to the two demoted "LAST ENUMERATOR" sentences at
          SGIRE_W_PRIM_OP and SGIRE_W_TEX_RD, both present in the enum.
   i. CODEX RECORDS AUDIT OF SECTION 6 (codex_pretable6_20260910.txt, NO-GO, six items; appended ~05:1x,
      after a first append of this subsection was truncated by the tool that wrote it and cut back to
      the "h." block before this complete copy was appended):
      1. THE DISCLOSURE IN 6.f RESTED ON A FALSE COST PREMISE -- ADOPTED, L1.  "One session per bit" was
         wrong: ONE session primes all twelve other bits before the colour decline, and the tree already
         holds the precedent (sgi_re_width_probe.py's `tex xbit' row, CLASS CLOSURE ON THE LATCH-COLLISION
         AXIS, which proved this priming sequence fires bits 0-10; its `tex rd lat1' row proves the 64-bit
         TEX read fires bit 11).  Rows added: `allbits primed' (12/12, the row proves its own setup),
         `allbits announce' (1 -- a latch sharing ANY existing bit reads 0), `allbits paint' (control).
         The contract's disclosure sentence is REPLACED by the closure; M15_texrd is now EXPECTED KILLED
         and M18_sharedbit_tlba (a width bit) is added, both in table 2c on the 19-row instrument.  The
         existing sessions are untouched (one new session), so every earlier kill carries over per row.
      2. "THE FOURTEEN PRE-EXISTING SESSIONS" IN 6.g CONFUSED ROWS WITH SESSIONS -- CORRECTED.  There are
         NINE pre-existing sessions (t_live, t_ycrcb1, t_resv, t_ycrcb2, t_ci, t_rgb, t_abgr, t_two,
         t_ind) carrying fourteen rows; the repair adds one session (ten) for two rows (sixteen), and cut
         4 adds one more (eleven sessions, nineteen rows).  The identity claim SURVIVES the count
         correction: codex read setup_put, store, run_ops and Addr and found no old session's commands
         differ, and `A = Addr(CODE)' before t_ind keeps the inserted sessions from shifting its
         instruction addresses.
      3. "WORD 1'S LOW BYTE" IN 6.g WAS WRONG -- CORRECTED.  A four-byte store at byte 1 spans bytes 1-4;
         byte 4 is the second word's MOST significant byte (the destination is read big-endian), and an
         overlap is a POSSIBLE change, not a guaranteed red (the stored byte could equal the painted one).
         The first cut's (1,0) row would therefore have reddened by accident if at all; the (4,1) target
         is sound.
      4. THE CHRONOLOGY WAS CONTRADICTORY -- CORRECTED, AND THE ORDER PROOF RE-BASED.  The header's
         "written 2026-09-10 ~04:2x BEFORE table 1 runs (mtime is the proof)" carried a GUESSED time that
         was wrong: the file's mtime before any append was 03:51 (an `ls -la' in the session transcript);
         table 1's baseline copy is 04:06:17 and its first log 04:10:56.  The appends since have moved
         this file's mtime (04:27:50 after 6.h), so the mtime is no longer the proof.  THE PROOF IS THE
         VERBATIM COPY: sections 1-5 are inlined in PASS2_brief_20260910.md (mtime 04:00:19, and the
         panel dir's brief_sent.md 04:00:19), which predates the baseline copy by six minutes.  That is
         the #518 convention (proven by the copy in the brief, not by mtime) and the header's own
         "mtime is the proof" clause is WITHDRAWN.
      5. 6.f's "BODY-INDUCED PARTITION" JUSTIFICATION WAS WRONG -- WITHDRAWN, ROW KEPT ON A DIFFERENT
         WARRANT.  (0,0) and (4,1) pass the SAME bounds decisions; the tiled bounds check distinguishes
         permitted from rejected coordinates, not zero from non-zero, and the linear rejection is
         `(x >> 12) >= 32' through tile_nr, not the unparenthesised expression quoted.  The row's actual
         warrant: `if (ok || x)' is a one-token edit MEASURED to keep every existing row green (grok's
         Q2, codex confirmed) and, for a guest, it IS the guard removed -- almost every real pixel has a
         non-zero coordinate -- so a detector claiming to catch the guard removed while sampling only the
         origin claimed coverage it did not have (L1 on the contract's own "the `ok' guard REMOVED"
         sentence).  The joint-cells clause does not demand the row; the contract's own sentence does.
      6. 6.h NAMED THE WRONG PRECEDING DEMOTIONS -- CORRECTED.  "the two demotions above it" sits inside
         the SGIRE_W_TEX_RD comment and refers to SGIRE_W_PRIM_OP's and SGIRE_W_BADMODE's demoted
         last-enumerator sentences (both present); TEX_RD's is the current demotion, not one of the two.
      Also confirmed by the audit: 6.b's counts (3 hunks, 48 additions with 3 blanks, 13 deletions, 61
      total, 58 non-blank, against 1ce8296); 6.a's red set under `int ok = 0' is exactly `live', `ci8 put',
      `rgb put', `abgr put' with `live silent' unchanged; the width-qualified header and 6.c-e.
   j. CODEX CHECK OF THE `allbits' ROWS AGAINST THE SOURCE (codex_allbits_20260910.txt, NO-GO, 1 item;
      appended ~05:5x, before table 2c copied its instrument).  CONFIRMED by reading the dispatch: all
      twelve priming accesses reach their sites (its table names each arm by grep anchor), every PRIME_LIT
      entry is an exact substring of the emitted text, none of the accesses dies, the priming does not
      obstruct the YCRCB PUT (the byte store to LINEAR_A never reaches the array; a (0,0)-(0,0) RECT really
      iterates once; the 0xf8 drawmode disables XFER/ROP/stipple), and the warn mask progresses
      0x1ff -> 0x5ff -> 0x7ff -> 0xfff so no primed bit is clear when the default arm runs: aliasing
      BADCOLOR to ANY bit 0-11 gives count 0.  THE ONE ITEM: the session sends 197 debugger commands with
      fixed 0.22 s read windows and a 4.0 s final spin, and `rd()' neither waits for a prompt nor extends
      its deadline while output arrives -- so a delayed-but-healthy emulator can leave the final `print v0'
      unread and the paint row reads `absent'.  DISPOSITION: recorded as a LOAD SENSITIVITY of the
      instrument's session shape (every wall-clock oracle in this tree has it), in the SAFE direction -- a
      loud false RED on the fix, never a false green; the HEAD smoke run (three tables concurrent) completed
      its transcript (12/12 primed, the paint row read a value).  No instrument change; the block comment
      says so.  Codex's other caution -- `allbits primed' checks presence, not order -- is answered by the
      source trace it gave (the order is fixed by the command list) and by `once', which covers a setter
      mutation the three rows alone would not.
